## 10. Variable Selection Gate (VSG) [M]

- **VSG-1** Per **target** and per variable, the user sets one **Inclusion State**:

  | State | Meaning |
  |---|---|
  | `force_include` | Must remain in the model unless it violates a leakage or integrity rule (the conflict is shown, never silently overridden) |
  | `candidate` | Eligible; AUTO feature selection decides |
  | `analysis_only` | Included in EDA, tests and mining; never a model predictor |
  | `exclude` | Excluded from tests and modeling, but still listed in the data dictionary |

- **VSG-2** Default state is **AUTO**. AUTO resolves from role, constant/near-constant checks, missing rate, leakage flags and availability, each logged with signals and rule (AUTO-2 format).
- **VSG-3** The gate shows, **per variable**, a pre-test profile: type, unit, missing %, unique count, variance, constant/near-constant flag, leakage suspicion, `known_in_advance` flag and a mini-histogram. It also shows an **Impact Preview**: number of variables, number of tests to run, multiple-testing family sizes, estimated compute time (Fast Mode vs Full Mode).
- **VSG-4** Bulk actions: select by role/type/name pattern/missing-rate range; invert; copy one target’s selection to another; **save and load selection presets** (JSON, reusable across projects).
- **VSG-5** **Test scope per variable group:** the user may restrict which test families run on which variables (see TCP-8).
- **VSG-6** **Integrity checks are not disableable:** leakage rules (MT-2), time-order checks, DST/duplicate-timestamp handling, target-never-in-predictors. The gate shows a conflict if the user forces something that violates them.
- **VSG-7** **Selection provenance:** every selection records `source` (`AUTO` | `USER_OVERRIDE`), timestamp and **`phase`** (`pre_test` | `post_test`). A change made after test results are visible is flagged `post_hoc` and listed under Limitations (“data-dependent selection”). A selection made before results is `pre_registered`.
- **VSG-8** Changing the selection after a run **invalidates only dependent stages** (via the cache key in TCP-10) and shows which stages will recompute.
- **VSG-9 [S]** Excluded variables can optionally get a **descriptive-only profile** (no inferential tests) so that nothing about them is hidden.

