## 13. Test Configuration & Decision Policy (TCP) [M]

- **TCP-1 Global settings** (all default AUTO, all overridable, all logged as `USER_OVERRIDE`):
  - significance level **α** (allowed range 0.001–0.20; values > 0.10 show a warning; presets: Strict 0.01 / Standard 0.05 [AUTO default] / Exploratory 0.10 / Custom);
  - **confidence level** (80/90/95/99 %, Custom within 50–99.9 %; default 95 %), which also drives the UQ coverage default (UQ-2);
  - **multiple-testing correction**: none | Bonferroni | Holm | Benjamini–Hochberg | Benjamini–Yekutieli. Raw **and** adjusted p-values are always stored;
  - **correction family definition**: per test type × per target (default) | per target | global | per variable;
  - **decision rule**: `p_only` | `p_and_effect_size` (default) | `ci_based` | `equivalence` (TOST).
- **TCP-2 Per-test overrides:** every test has its own parameter panel with validated ranges and tooltips explaining each parameter. At minimum:
  - α and correction (inherit or override);
  - **alternative** (`two-sided` | `greater` | `less`) where applicable;
  - **max lags** (fixed | AIC | BIC | Schwert rule), **deterministic terms** (`n`/`c`/`ct`/`ctt` for ADF/KPSS), **HAC kernel and bandwidth**;
  - **minimum sample size** (below it the test is skipped with a logged reason);
  - **effect-size thresholds** (|r|, Cohen's d, Cliff's δ, η², Cramér's V, SMD) with small/medium/large cut points;
  - **equivalence margin** for TOST;
  - **bootstrap settings** (method, block length AUTO, replicates ≥ 1,000 AUTO, seed).
- **TCP-3 Test selection tree:** the user can enable/disable each test family and each test, with dependencies shown (e.g., Granger requires stationarity checks), a cost estimate and a “why you might keep this” hint. A disabled test appears in the report as **“not run (user)”**, never omitted. Disabling warns if downstream stages rely on it.
- **TCP-4 Expected-sign and monotonicity constraints:** per predictor per target: `+` | `−` | `free` | `non-monotonic allowed`, optionally with a tolerance. Used for coefficient **sign checks** (parametric), **monotonic constraints** (LightGBM/XGBoost where supported), **PDP/ALE plausibility** (non-parametric), and the physical-plausibility score (SC-1). Violations are reported with magnitude and CI, never silently corrected.
- **TCP-5 Gate role per test result:** each test has a role the user can change: `informational` (reported only); `warning` (UI/report flag); `gating` (triggers a downstream action: difference, flag, exclude from the Candidate Pool, switch imputation, change CV scheme). Downstream actions follow an **Action Policy**: `flag_only` | `ask_user` (default for destructive actions) | `auto_apply` (always logged). **No test result ever deletes data silently.**
- **TCP-6 What-if re-evaluation:** because statistics and p-values are stored, changing α, correction, decision rule or effect-size thresholds **re-evaluates decisions instantly without recomputing** the statistic. The UI shows a diff of flipped decisions. Parameters that change the statistic itself (lags, trend spec, bootstrap) recompute only the affected tests.
- **TCP-7 Decision sensitivity table:** for every test, show the decision at α ∈ {0.01, 0.05, 0.10}, flagging **“decision flips”** between levels. The report summarizes how many conclusions are threshold-sensitive.
- **TCP-8 Test scope matrix:** a variables × test-families grid (per target) where the user ticks which tests apply to which variables. Default AUTO from role and type.
- **TCP-9 [M] Pre-registration mode:** the user may lock the Test Configuration **before running** it. The lock is hashed and stored. Any later change is labeled `post_hoc` in the report. **The default AUTO configuration is considered pre-registered if accepted before results are shown**; the UI must make this explicit.
- **TCP-10 Result caching:** results are keyed by `(data_hash, variable_set, test_id, parameters, seed, library_versions)`. The cache enables TCP-6 and VSG-8 and is part of reproducibility.
- **TCP-11 Guard rails:** validate every input (type, range, cross-parameter consistency). Warn when a configuration is statistically unsound (e.g., high α with no correction across hundreds of tests; ADF max-lag larger than n/3; a normality test on n > 5,000 with a p-only decision). Warnings are shown, the user may proceed (logged).
- **TCP-12 Effect size and practical significance:** with `p_and_effect_size`, a result is “significant” only if p_adj < α **and** the effect size exceeds its threshold. The report distinguishes **statistical** from **practical** significance.
- **TCP-13 Configuration snapshot:** the full effective configuration (AUTO-resolved and user values) is saved in the project, exported with reports and included in the Reproducibility section.
- **TCP-14 [S] Bayesian alternative:** when the user selects a Bayesian decision framework, the engine may report Bayes factors or posterior probabilities of a region of practical equivalence (ROPE) instead of p-values. **Never mix Bayesian and frequentist evidence in the same comparison table without clear labels.**

