## 14. Comprehensive Test Catalog [M]

Each test emits: statistic, p-value (**raw and adjusted**), df, effect size (where defined), CI, n used, assumptions checked, decision, interpretation, downstream action, `mode`.

**Implementation honesty [M]:** `statsmodels` provides ADF, KPSS, Granger, Ljung–Box, Breusch–Godfrey, Breusch–Pagan, White, RESET, ARCH-LM, Jarque–Bera, Cook's D, TOST and more. **Phillips–Perron, DF-GLS and Zivot–Andrews come from `arch`** (verify). **Little's MCAR** is available via `statsmodels.imputation.mice.MICEData(df).mcar_test()`. **Chow, Quandt–Andrews, Pettitt, Diebold–Mariano, Nemenyi, and other missing functions are custom** (or from a verified package such as `pychow`). **Every custom test MUST be validated against R reference values** (`strucchange`, `MissMech`, `tseries`, `urca`, `forecast`, `Kendall`, etc.). Anything not shipped goes into the Not-Implemented Register.

| ID | Family | Tests and priority |
|---|---|---|
| **T1** | Distribution & normality | **[M]** Shapiro–Wilk (n ≤ 5,000), D'Agostino–Pearson, Anderson–Darling, Lilliefors, Jarque–Bera, skewness/kurtosis z-tests, QQ/PP plots |
| **T2** | Outliers (flag only) | **[M]** Tukey fences, Hampel/MAD, Grubbs, Generalized ESD (Rosner), STL-residual, robust Mahalanobis (MCD), IsolationForest, LOF · **[O]** Dixon |
| **T3** | Group / regime comparison | **[M]** Welch t, Mann–Whitney, one-way and Welch ANOVA, Kruskal–Wallis; post-hoc Tukey HSD, Games–Howell, Dunn; Levene/Brown–Forsythe, Bartlett, Fligner–Killeen; 2-sample KS, k-sample Anderson–Darling, Cramér–von Mises; paired t, Wilcoxon signed-rank, sign test |
| **T4** | Association | **[M]** Pearson, Spearman, Kendall τ-b, distance correlation, mutual information, partial and semi-partial correlation, point-biserial, correlation ratio η, Cramér's V, χ², Fisher exact, lagged cross-correlation · **[O]** MIC, Hoeffding's D |
| **T5** | Stationarity / unit roots | **[M]** ADF (deterministic-term selection), KPSS (c, ct), PP · **[S]** DF-GLS, Zivot–Andrews (one break) · **[O]** seasonal unit-root tests (OCSB/Canova–Hansen; verify availability) |
| **T6** | Trend, seasonality, randomness | **[M]** Mann–Kendall (autocorrelation-modified and seasonal variants), Sen's slope, seasonal-subseries Kruskal–Wallis, Friedman, STL/MSTL seasonal and trend strength, runs test, Ljung–Box on the raw series · **[S]** Cox–Stuart, QS test, Fisher g periodogram test, turning-point, Bartels rank, BDS nonlinearity |
| **T7** | Multivariate structure | **[M]** KMO, Bartlett sphericity, VIF, condition number, eigenvalue analysis, Granger (on stationary series, corrected) · **[S]** Engle–Granger and Johansen cointegration, Mardia/Henze–Zirkler normality · **[O]** transfer entropy |
| **T8** | Missingness | **[M]** Little's MCAR (`statsmodels` MICE), pattern analysis (matrix, co-occurrence), logistic test of missingness vs covariates, gap run-length distribution |
| **T9** | Structural stability | **[M]** Chow (custom, HAC option), Quandt–Andrews sup-F (custom, bootstrap p-values), CUSUM, CUSUM-SQ, Pettitt, PELT/BinSeg · **[S]** Bai–Perron, ICSS (variance breaks), SNHT · **[O]** Hansen instability, Bayesian online change-point |
| **T10** | Regression diagnostics (post-model, OOF residuals) | **[M]** residual normality, Durbin–Watson, Ljung–Box, Breusch–Godfrey, Breusch–Pagan, White, Goldfeld–Quandt, ARCH-LM, RESET, Harvey–Collier, Rainbow; Cook's D (flag > 4/n), DFFITS, DFBETAS, leverage, studentized residuals; coefficient t/F/Wald with HAC SEs; partial F; LR test for nested models; **sign checks (TCP-4)**. For non-parametric ML models these are descriptive only (state this). |
| **T11** | Model comparison | **[M]** Diebold–Mariano (Harvey–Leybourne–Newbold correction), corrected resampled t-test (Nadeau–Bengio), Wilcoxon on fold errors, Friedman + Nemenyi, paired bootstrap CI of metric differences · **[S]** TOST model equivalence, Model Confidence Set |
| **T12** | Interval calibration | **[M]** Kupiec POF, Christoffersen independence/conditional coverage, binomial coverage test, rolling coverage |
| **T13** | Power & sample size | **[S]** minimum detectable effect and a-priori power analysis (never “observed power”) |
| **T14** | M&V balance (only with a `group` column) | **[O]** TOST with configurable margin, SMD < 0.1 + Love plot, parallel-trends test, propensity overlap, kernel balancing |

**Mandatory test-specific logic [M]:**

- **TC-1 Assumptions:** every test declares its assumptions (independence, normality, stationarity, minimum n). The engine checks them and, if violated, **auto-switches to a robust or non-parametric variant (logged)** or marks the result “assumption violated”.
- **TC-2 Autocorrelation-aware inference:** correlation and mean-comparison tests on time series use HAC or **block-bootstrap / effective-sample-size** inference. Classical p-values for autocorrelated series are labeled “naive”.
- **TC-3** Never claim causation from Granger, cointegration or correlation. Granger is used for **candidate lag screening only**, on stationary series, with correction.
- **TC-4 Stationarity decision matrix (T5):** ADF rejects & KPSS does not → stationary; ADF does not reject & KPSS rejects → non-stationary → difference or flag; both reject → trend-stationary or structural break suspected; neither rejects → inconclusive (low power). Differencing is applied only if the model family needs it, and is logged.
- **TC-5 Structural breaks (T9):** Chow applies when the break date is known; Quandt–Andrews when unknown. A baseline-vs-reporting Chow requires actuals in the reporting window. **In M&V, a detected break is reported as “possible savings or non-routine event”, never as automatic model failure.**
- **TC-6 Missingness (T8):** Little's MCAR assumes multivariate normality; interpret cautiously for time series. If MCAR is rejected or inconclusive, default to **time-aware** imputation (bounded interpolation, iterative imputer with lags) plus missingness indicators. MNAR is untestable; document it.
- **TC-7 Influence (T2/T10):** flag only; removal requires a logged Action Policy (DAT-8).
- **TC-8 Large-n behavior:** with very large n nearly every p-value is significant, so effect size and practical thresholds (TCP-12) are mandatory in the display. Large-n tests (distance correlation, Shapiro–Wilk) use documented subsampling or approximate variants, logged.

